what's the difference between angel and  venture capital investors it's a great  question and one that came in from one  of my YouTube viewers  JGI g7 284 thank you on YouTube and it's  really important I remember when I had  my first business I felt like I needed  help and I felt like I needed funding  and I didn't know if I needed to go to  an angel investor or venture capital I  didn't know how much money they wanted  to spend they didn't know who would be a  good fit for me and the problem is if if  you don't know the difference and if you  don't know who you should bring on it  could not only waste your time like  raising capital is almost a full-time  job for your business and so you don't  want to spend your time going in the  wrong direction but also bringing on the  wrong person can really sink your  company raising money really is like  getting married you have to really pick  who you bring on because again the wrong  person could destroy your company and  everything that you've worked so hard to  build the vision everything that you  believe in could be torn apart because  you brought the wrong person on board so  really tread carefully and do your  homework so here are the main  differences an angel investor is an  individual they're one person they  either do it as a profession so they are  a professional angel investor they're  sophisticated they'd like invested in  the companies as their main business but  there's also a lot of people who are not  professional who are unsophisticated who  have no idea what they're doing they're  wealthy individuals there's an industry  term called DDI which is doctors or  dentists with disposable income a lot of  these ancient Buster's are like dentists  who hate their lives who don't find  satisfaction in their in their career  anymore and they invest in companies as  a way to tell their friends about the  things that they're doing and and live  an exciting life through you they're not  professional investors and so angels are  all over the map and you really want to  do your homework to find the ones that  will give you not just money but  connections experience ideas wisdom  beyond that you never want to bring on  money just for money you need somebody  who can bring more to the  table and also who believes in what  you're trying to do they don't just see  an opportunity to make cash but they  believe that the change that you're  trying to make in this world is a good  one  it's super important typically angel  investors do have a higher tolerance for  risk they are looking at a longer  horizon typically five to eight years  before they're gonna get their money  back so they're they're in it more for  the long term and if you're looking at  raising less than half a million dollars  for your business and you're probably  dealing in the angel investor territory  so the angels are the the first capital  that gets you going  it could also be your friends and family  or your own startup money but if you're  going for like a professional investor  then you're going after an angel if you  need half a million dollars or less the  next step up is looking at VCS and  venture capitalists typically we'll look  at half a million dollars plus the  reason is it's the same amount of work  to do a fifteen million dollar deal as a  half a million dollar deal and so VCS  don't like to spend their time working  on these small deals because the payoff  isn't there they'll only do it if they  really see a lot of opportunity with the  company and they'll be involved in  future rounds so they'll work with you  on a small scale because they think they  can take you through a couple rounds of  financing and by getting in with you  early then they'll you'll want to go  back with them you know they they're  they're getting their foot in the door  at the start but typically VCS don't  like startup companies pure startup it's  really really hard to get them they want  to see some momentum with your business  some traction they want to see a little  bit of revenue at least to show that hey  you've done it this far with some money  you can go beyond typically they're  looking at a shorter horizon so they  want three to five years and they're  gonna exit they'll never invest to buy  out and in another investor so if you  had a really bad angel investor and now  you're trying to raise more money to buy  out that angel they're not going to do  it this is why it's important right you  get the wrong investor they're stuck  with you forever it's really really  really hard to get rid of them and  nobody's gonna want to come in just to  buy them out with VCS they want to see  that there's a clear exit strategy so  the two most common ones are  you're gonna go public so there's a  there's an IPO your company goes public  or you get acquired so some big  company's gonna buy you up and pay you a  good price for it  the third less common one is a  management buyout so you buy back the  shares from the VCS but they don't want  to see that they want to see massive  growth they want to see that you're  gonna go out and build something  humongous make tons of money and  someone's going to want to buy you up  VCS are professional they pool money so  they have people who give them money and  then they're in charge of going out and  investing it and they typically focus on  specific areas angels are all over the  map again some will invest in an area  that they have expertise in and others  will just invest because they think they  can make money but with VCS they're  usually really targeted so they want to  see a tech company or they want to see a  green company or whatever it is they  have specific criteria that they're  looking for VCS are looking to make  thirty percent a year return on their  investment and it's not going to come in  the first year second year but by the  time they exit after three to five years  they want to see that made 30% per year  on their investment so you have to have  a type of company that will grow that  quickly where angels they want to make  money but it's not as set right so they  may invest in something that they  believe in personally they have some  kind of affinity with but it's not going  to yield as big a return because they  like it and they're okay with a lesser  return maybe there's more safety to it  where VC wants to see massive rapid  growth and so it lends itself to  software companies to technology  companies because it's easier to scale  those types of businesses if you look at  a consulting business for example a VC  wouldn't touch that because it's it's  too hard to scale quickly the returns  aren't there we're an angel investor  might invest in that kind of business  because they see a way to make a little  bit of money and they may believe  strongly in what you're doing so  hopefully that helps with some of the  main differences again when you are  looking at raising funds I would start  by thinking about how much money do you  need and a half a million dollars a good  mark if it's less you're looking at an  angel that's more you're looking at  Abby's  but really scrutinize the person that  you're bringing on because I know it's  exciting to get money right I know it's  exciting finally somebody believes in us  finally we're able to rapidly grow this  thing up you know you've been struggling  and grinding it out and and just making  ends meet and not paying yourself very  well and finally somebody believes in  you and there's so much excitement  around that but it's easy to get locked  into a bad deal everybody's on their  best behavior when things are going well  especially at the beginning but when  things get rough which they're gonna get  rough with your business you need to  have somebody there who will still  support you who will really still  believe in what you're trying to do with  your business and so I just urge you to  scrutinize strongly everybody that  you're bringing on as a potential  partner or investor into your business  because if you do make the wrong  decision that could really sink your  company they need to be bringing more to  the table than just money  they need to belief in your business and  they need to have some kind of expertise  that they can share or contacts  experience something that helps your  business go beyond just the money great  question I hope that helped for those of  you watching you like the video please  give it a thumbs up below it always  makes me want to do more for you guys  and I love the Hurriyet to think please  leave a comment underneath let me know  what you're thinking about this video  and if you want me to do a video  response for you you have a business  problem leave it below I'll create the  video thank you guys I'll see you soon
 

